CORE
main.cpp
--cpp14
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ explicit this: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ member function call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ generic lambda: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ capture-default: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ modifies the object: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.prim.this]/2, [class.mfct.non.static]/2,
[expr.prim.lambda.capture]/10: in a lambda capturing `this', `this' and an
unqualified member function call denote the enclosing object.  Such lambdas
were not given a closure (only data-member odr-uses were modelled, as
reference members); a generic one was lowered to a function pointer with
`int' for `auto', so the call with a class argument computed the wrong value
(bin53).  The closure now keeps the captured pointer (lambda_this_capture)
and the body's `this' and member calls use it (rewrite_lambda_this_uses).
libstdc++ <format>'s _Formatting_scanner::_M_format_arg visitor (format:3648)
is such a lambda.  The generic lambda needs C++14.
g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept it, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
